a ratio full:removed=4:1; centroids full=(2/3,2/3,2/3), removed=(4/3,1/3,1/3), giving centroid=(4/9,7/9,7/9). Will draft answer.md now; please review before we mark final.\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Draft /work/answer.md written with full conditional-slice proof, simplified iff condition, zero-value cases, and counterexample whose nearest point has no binding value caps. Please independently review and append agreement/corrections; I will validate numerically.\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22: Numerical check of exact inclusion-exclusion centroid formula matched theorem on 29,907 random positive-value cases away from regime thresholds; example centroid independently confirmed (4/9,7/9,7/9). Added formulas for the common point in equality regimes. Proof remains self-contained. Awaiting argon review before FINAL.\n# Exact condition\n\nWrite \\(r=v_P\\), \\(S=v_A+v_B+v_C\\), and \\(a=\\min(v_A,v_B,v_C)\\). Values are taken to be nonnegative, as usual. The centroid means the uniform area centroid, or the uniform length centroid for a segment.\n\nIf all three winner values are positive, then\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\quad\np^{\\mathrm{VN}}=\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nv_A=v_B=v_C\\quad\\text{or}\\quad r\\le a\\quad\\text{or}\\quad r\\ge S-a.\n\\quad}\n\\]\nIn the regime \\(r\\le a\\), the common point is \\((r/3,r/3,r/3)\\). In the regime \\(r\\ge S-a\\), it is \\((v_A-D/3,v_B-D/3,v_C-D/3)\\), where \\(D=S-r\\). If all three winner values are equal, the common point is again \\((r/3,r/3,r/3)\\).\n\nThe assumption \\(r<S\\) remains in force. If a winner has value zero, the two points always agree; the face then has dimension at most one. Thus, allowing zero values, add \\(a=0\\) to the alternatives in the box.\n\n# Proof\n\n## 1. Reduce the core and shift by VCG\n\nThe displayed core inequalities in the question are equivalent to\n\\[\n0\\le p_i\\le v_i,\\qquad \\sum_i p_i\\ge r.\n\\]\nIndeed, the inequality for all winners gives the revenue bound. Conversely, for an